A||||7||Adrift||is it a Bad idea to use pink fibreglass house insulation in my fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||12-30-2001||09:36 PM||||I would like to add some more insulation to my fiero for temperature & sound dampening. I can go to home depot and for $20 get enough of that pink fibreglass "pink pather"  insulation to do the whole car. Are there any problems with this? I'm thinking of puting it under the carpets, behind the dash, doors, etc...||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||12-30-2001||09:46 PM||||Did you ever get any of that stuff on you?<BR>It'll itch like hell. Especially if it "sheds".<BR>I've heard that Home Depot sells rubber roof membrane that is similar to Dynamat, but much cheaper. Might be worth a look.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||88CoupeV6||12-30-2001||09:56 PM||||I would not use that. JC Whitney has automotive insulation that would be a better choice.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||3
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||10:12 PM||||we have a precut kit that comes in 6 peices that is $80<P> It is 1/2" sound absorption/ heat reflective material that can reduce cabin temps and reduces sound by nearly 10db<P> We have yet to post the pics on the web site, however on another thread I posted a pic with a polished intake and valve covers, that car has the kit on its firewall and is visible.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||12-30-2001||10:16 PM||||I bought some insulation at Lowe's that has worked great in our car projects. It looks like packing bubble wrap but it is two layers of bubbles and has a foil facing on both sides. It is about 1/4 of an inch thick. We used blueboard glue to put it in roof, quarters, doors and floor of our '47 Ford street rod project and it worked great. I used the leftovers to put behind the firewall carpet and under the seats in my Fiero. I also put a piece between the sunroof glass and sunshade panel. It is more quiet and easier to heat than before. The good thing is no itching!||||reg||1
Z||000005||rodmcneill||12-30-2001||10:22 PM||||West Coast Fiero, where does the 6 piece kit go?||||reg||5
Z||000006||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||10:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rodmcneill:<BR><B>West Coast Fiero, where does the 6 piece kit go?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>they are 6 self adhesive peices that adhere to the fire wall within the engine bay. They are in six peices as such to prevent romaval of every component from the firewall for instalation<P>I will have pics posted shortly<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-31-2001||01:52 PM||||it is itchy stuff. but inside its a cheap alternative. We used it everywhere on custom van conversions. I wouldnt use it anywhere thats exposed to weather though, like in engine bay. And Id not put it inside doors, it will tangle up in hardware inside there.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-31-2001).]||||reg||1
